<p class="lead">How much does it cost to replace a roof on a 1,500 square foot house? For most Central Texas homes with architectural asphalt shingles, plan on a total somewhere around $8,500 to $15,000 for a single-story house. A two-story house with the same living space often comes in lower. The gap is wide because "1,500 square feet" describes your floor plan, while roofers price by the area of the roof surface, and those two numbers are rarely the same.</p>

<p>This guide works through the math the way a roofing estimator would. We measure the roof in squares, add the effect of pitch and waste, and then apply typical material and labor rates. Then we cover the Austin-specific factors that move the final number: summer heat, spring hail, city permits, HOA rules and the condition of the decking under your old shingles. When you want a number for your own house, the <a href="/roofing-tools/roof-cost-calculator/">roof cost calculator</a> runs the same steps with your measurements.</p>

<h2 id="h-average-roof-replacement-costs-for-a-1-500-square-foot-home">Average Cost to Replace a Roof on a 1,500 Square Foot House</h2>
<p>Here is the short version for a typical Austin-area single-story home with about 1,500 sq ft of living space and a moderate 6/12 pitch. These are planning ranges based on common industry pricing. They are not a quote, and actual prices change with material costs, roof complexity and the season.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
  <thead><tr><th>Roofing material</th><th>Typical installed cost per square</th><th>Estimated total (≈20 squares)</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>3-tab asphalt shingles</td><td>$375–$550</td><td>$7,500–$11,000</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Architectural (dimensional) shingles</td><td>$425–$700</td><td>$8,500–$14,000+</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Class 4 impact-resistant shingles</td><td>$550–$850</td><td>$11,000–$17,000</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Exposed-fastener metal panels</td><td>$600–$950</td><td>$12,000–$19,000</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Stone-coated steel</td><td>$850–$1,300</td><td>$17,000–$26,000</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Standing seam metal</td><td>$950–$1,500</td><td>$19,000–$30,000</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Concrete tile</td><td>$900–$1,500</td><td>$18,000–$30,000</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Clay tile / natural slate</td><td>$1,500–$3,000+</td><td>$30,000–$60,000+</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table></div>
<p>A two-story version of the same house might only need 10–12 squares, so the material-driven part of the bill drops by close to half. Two-story work still needs more safety setup and more trips up the ladder, so the price per square often runs a little higher.</p>

<h2 id="h-understanding-roofing-measurements-and-surface-area">Why 1,500 Square Feet of House Is Not 1,500 Square Feet of Roof</h2>
<p>Roofers measure in <a href="/blog/what-is-a-square-in-roofing/">roofing squares</a>. One square is 100 square feet of roof surface. To get from living space to roof squares, you have to account for four things the floor plan leaves out.</p>
<ul class="checklist">
  <li><strong>Footprint vs. living space.</strong> A two-story home spreads its 1,500 sq ft over two floors, so the roof only covers about 750 sq ft of footprint. A single-story home's roof covers all 1,500, plus any attached garage or covered porch under the same roofline.</li>
  <li><strong>Overhangs.</strong> Eaves and rakes usually extend 12–24 inches past the walls. On a typical ranch home that adds roughly 10–15% to the area.</li>
  <li><strong>Pitch.</strong> A sloped surface has more area than the flat footprint beneath it. A 6/12 roof has about 11.8% more area than its footprint, and a 12/12 roof has about 41% more.</li>
  <li><strong>Waste.</strong> Shingles get cut at hips, valleys and rakes. Estimators add about 10% for a simple gable roof and 15% or more for a cut-up hip roof.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Worked example: one story vs. two story</h3>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
  <thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>1-story, 1,500 sq ft</th><th>2-story, 1,500 sq ft</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Roof footprint</td><td>1,500 sq ft</td><td>750 sq ft</td></tr>
    <tr><td>+ overhangs (~12%)</td><td>1,680 sq ft</td><td>840 sq ft</td></tr>
    <tr><td>× 6/12 pitch factor (1.118)</td><td>1,878 sq ft</td><td>939 sq ft</td></tr>
    <tr><td>+ waste (10–15%)</td><td>2,066–2,160 sq ft</td><td>1,033–1,080 sq ft</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Squares to order</strong></td><td><strong>≈21–22</strong></td><td><strong>≈10–11</strong></td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table></div>
<p>Add an attached two-car garage (around 400–450 sq ft of footprint) and the one-story roof can pass 26 squares. Steepen the pitch to 8/12 and the factor becomes 1.202, which adds about another square and a half. That is why two houses listed at the same square footage on Zillow can get roof quotes thousands of dollars apart. The calculator below asks for your pitch, and if you are unsure, a roofer can measure it during an inspection.</p>

<h2 id="h-roofing-material-options-and-cost-differences">Roofing Material Options and How They Change the Price</h2>
<p>Material choice is the biggest single lever on a roof replacement in Austin. It also decides how long the roof lasts in Texas heat and how well it stands up to hail.</p>

<h3 id="h-asphalt-shingles">Asphalt Shingles (3-Tab)</h3>
<p>Three-tab shingles are the thin, flat, single-layer product found on many older Austin homes. They cost the least, but they are rated for lower winds, look flat, and in Central Texas sun they often wear out in 12–18 years. Many manufacturers have cut back their 3-tab lines, and few homeowners choose them for a full replacement today.</p>

<h3 id="h-architectural-shingles">Architectural Shingles</h3>
<p>Architectural (dimensional or laminated) shingles are the standard asphalt shingle roof in Austin neighborhoods. Two bonded layers give them a thicker, shadowed look, higher wind ratings and better resistance to curling in heat. The asphalt shingle roof cost premium over 3-tab is usually modest, often $50–$150 per square. Class 4 impact-resistant versions cost more, but many Texas insurers offer premium discounts for them, so ask your agent before you choose. Our <a href="/blog/architectural-shingle-vs-asphalt-shingles/">architectural vs. 3-tab comparison</a> covers the trade-offs in detail.</p>

<h3 id="h-metal-roofing">Metal Roofing</h3>
<p>Metal is increasingly popular in the Hill Country and on newer Austin builds. Exposed-fastener panels are the budget metal option. Standing seam, with hidden fasteners and floating clips, costs more but handles heat expansion and wind very well and can last 40–60 years. Reflective finishes also cut attic heat gain. Ask about profile and finish options when you get quotes.</p>

<h3 id="h-tile-wood-and-specialty-roofs">Tile, Wood and Specialty Roofs</h3>
<p>Concrete and clay tile suit Spanish and Mediterranean-style homes and last a very long time, but they are heavy, and the framing may need an engineer's review before switching to tile. Wood shakes are uncommon in Austin because of fire concerns and the upkeep they need in humid weather. Slate and synthetic slate sit at the top of the price range.</p>

<h2 id="h-labor-costs-and-regional-pricing-differences">Labor Costs and What Drives Them</h2>
<p>Labor usually accounts for roughly 40–60% of a residential roof replacement. Crews price labor per square, then adjust for:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Pitch.</strong> Anything steeper than about 7/12 needs roof jacks, harnesses and slower work, so expect a per-square surcharge that rises with each step in slope.</li>
  <li><strong>Stories and access.</strong> Two-story eaves, tight side yards, pools and landscaping all slow material handling.</li>
  <li><strong>Complexity.</strong> Every valley, dormer, skylight and wall junction adds flashing work.</li>
  <li><strong>Season.</strong> After a major spring hailstorm, demand across Austin, Round Rock and Pflugerville spikes and schedules fill up. Booking ahead of hail season, or during the quieter late-fall months, gives you more scheduling flexibility.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="h-roof-tear-off-and-disposal-expenses">Tear-Off and Disposal</h2>
<p>Most quotes include removing one layer of old shingles. A second layer, which shows up on older homes in central and East Austin, adds labor, weight and dumpster fees, often roughly $50–$100 or more per square. Tile and heavy materials cost more to remove and haul. A good crew also protects landscaping and finishes with a magnetic nail sweep of the yard and driveway. Austin Roofing does this on every job.</p>

<h2 id="h-decking-repairs-and-structural-issues">Decking Repairs and Structural Surprises</h2>
<p>Nobody can see the plywood or OSB deck until the old roof comes off. Long-term leaks, failed pipe boots and poor ventilation leave soft, delaminated sheets that have to be replaced before new underlayment goes down. Reputable estimates list a per-sheet price, commonly around $70–$120 per 4×8 sheet installed, so you know the cost ahead of time. Older homes with plank (skip-sheathed) decking may need to be re-sheathed completely before shingles can go on, and that is a significant line item.</p>

<h2 id="h-underlayment-flashing-and-protective-layers">Underlayment, Flashing and Protective Layers</h2>
<p>A roof is a system, and the layers under the shingles affect both cost and leak resistance:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Underlayment.</strong> Synthetic underlayment has largely replaced felt. Read our guide to <a href="/blog/roofing-underlayment-material-types/">roofing underlayment types</a>.</li>
  <li><strong>Self-adhered membrane</strong> (often called ice and water shield) in valleys, around penetrations and on low-slope sections.</li>
  <li><strong>Drip edge, starter strip and ridge cap.</strong> Required by most manufacturers' installation instructions.</li>
  <li><strong>Flashing.</strong> New step, counter and pipe flashing is better than reusing rusted metal.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="h-ventilation-and-energy-efficiency-considerations">Ventilation and Energy Efficiency</h2>
<p>A replacement is the cheapest time to fix attic ventilation. Adding ridge vent and clearing blocked soffits costs relatively little while the roof is open, and it lowers attic temperatures that can climb well past 140°F in an Austin summer. A cooler attic also protects the shingle warranty, because most manufacturers require proper ventilation. Learn more about <a href="/blog/attic-ventilation-upgrade-benefits/">attic ventilation upgrade benefits</a>.</p>

<h2 id="h-permits-codes-and-local-regulations">Permits, Codes and HOA Rules</h2>
<p>The City of Austin and many surrounding cities require a permit for a full roof replacement, and fees and inspection requirements vary by jurisdiction. Your contractor should know whether your address falls under Austin, Travis County, Williamson County or a smaller city like Cedar Park or Kyle. Many Austin-area HOAs also restrict shingle colors or materials, so get architectural approval before you order. Our <a href="/blog/roof-replacement-permit-requirements/">roof replacement permit guide</a> explains what is typically required.</p>

<h2 id="h-insurance-and-storm-damage-considerations">Insurance and Storm Damage</h2>
<p>If hail or wind damaged the roof, your homeowners policy may pay for much of the replacement after your deductible. Many Texas policies use a percentage wind/hail deductible, such as 1–2% of the dwelling coverage, so read yours carefully. Whether you get replacement cost or actual cash value also changes your out-of-pocket amount. Austin Roofing documents damage with photos and can meet your adjuster on the roof. We do not negotiate claims, but good documentation helps the adjuster see everything. Ask about it when you schedule your inspection.</p>

<h2 id="h-long-term-value-and-return-on-investment">Long-Term Value and Return on Investment</h2>
<p>The cheapest roof is not always the best value. Divide the installed cost by the years you expect it to last. An architectural shingle roof at $12,000 lasting 20 years costs $600 a year. A standing seam roof at $25,000 lasting 50 years costs $500 a year, before you count energy savings and possible insurance discounts. A new roof also helps resale, because buyers and their inspectors in the Austin market look closely at roof age.</p>

<h2 id="h-how-location-impacts-roofing-costs">Austin-Specific Factors That Affect Roof Replacement Cost</h2>
<p>Roof replacement in Austin has a few local quirks that national cost guides miss:</p>
<div class="feature-grid">
  <div class="feature"><h3>Heat and UV</h3><p>Long 100°F+ stretches age shingles faster, so investing in better shingles and ventilation pays off here.</p></div>
  <div class="feature"><h3>Hail season</h3><p>Spring storms along I-35 drive demand for impact-resistant shingles and create seasonal scheduling crunches.</p></div>
  <div class="feature"><h3>Tree debris</h3><p>Live oak and cedar debris clog valleys and gutters, so valley flashing and membrane quality matter.</p></div>
  <div class="feature"><h3>Hill Country pitches</h3><p>Steeper, more complex roofs in Dripping Springs, Leander and west Austin raise labor costs per square.</p></div>
</div>

<h2 id="h-getting-accurate-and-reliable-estimates">How to Get an Accurate Roof Replacement Estimate</h2>
<ol class="steps">
  <li><h3>Get the squares count in writing</h3><p>Ask each roofer how many squares they measured and what waste factor they used. Big differences between quotes usually start here.</p></li>
  <li><h3>Check the scope line by line</h3><p>Tear-off layers, underlayment type, drip edge, flashing, ventilation and the decking price per sheet should all be listed.</p></li>
  <li><h3>Confirm the product</h3><p>Brand, product line and color, not just "architectural shingles."</p></li>
  <li><h3>Ask about permits, cleanup and timeline</h3><p>Most residential replacements take 1–2 days once materials arrive.</p></li>
</ol>
<p>Austin Roofing offers free inspections and free, no-pressure written quotes for <a href="/services/roof-replacement/">roof replacement in Austin</a> and across Central Texas, including Round Rock, Georgetown, Pflugerville and Kyle.</p>

<h2 id="h-final-cost-expectations-for-homeowners">Bottom Line: What to Budget</h2>
<p>For a 1,500 sq ft single-story Austin home with architectural shingles, a realistic planning budget is about $8,500–$15,000. Set aside another 10% for decking or other surprises. A two-story home of the same size will usually land lower. Metal, tile and impact-resistant upgrades move you into higher brackets, but they can pay for themselves over a long ownership. To get a number for your own roof, start with the <a href="/roofing-tools/roof-cost-calculator/">roof cost calculator</a>, then <a href="/contact-us/">schedule a free inspection</a> so we can measure and confirm it.</p>
